Set one variable in the env file, in place.

Only that line changes: its position, comments, `export` prefix and quoting stay. A name that is not in the file is added at the end. The file is written atomically, keeps its mode, and is kept as a `.bak` copy first. Nothing else is touched: no template, contract or .gitignore.

Without a value, it is read from standard input when that is piped, and asked for when there is a terminal. A name that looks secret (`API_TOKEN`, `DB_PASSWORD`) is asked for with no echo, and a value typed on the command line works but warns: your shell keeps it.

`--from-file PATH` (or `-` for standard input) reads the value from a file: a certificate, a key, a service-account JSON. Several lines are fine; the value is never printed.

`--fill` goes through every name in the file whose value is empty or a placeholder (`YOUR_KEY_HERE`, `changeme`) and asks for each one: a secret-looking name with no echo, Enter to skip. Nothing is written until the last answer, in one write with one `.bak`. It needs a terminal (exit 4 without one); `--dry-run` lists the names it would ask for. It sets names that are in the file; one that is only in the template is added by `evnx sync --direction reverse`.

A value that is replaced is confirmed first, unless --yes. With no terminal and no value on stdin, the exit code is 4.

Usage: evnx set [OPTIONS] [NAME[=VALUE]]

Arguments:
  [NAME[=VALUE]]
          The variable, as NAME or NAME=VALUE

Options:
      --if-missing
          Do nothing when the name is already in the file

      --fill
          Ask for every name whose value is empty or a placeholder

      --only <GLOB>
          With --fill: only names matching these globs (comma-separated or repeated)

  -n, --dry-run
          Show what would change; write nothing. Needs no terminal.

      --no-color
          Disable colored output

  -q, --quiet
          Print nothing on success. Findings, warnings, errors and requested data still print

  -v, --verbose
          Enable verbose output

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Files:
      --env <FILE>
          Env file to use. Defaults to `.env`

      --env-name <NAME>
          Use `.env.<NAME>` instead of `.env`

  -C, --dir <DIR>
          Project folder to work in. Defaults to the current folder

Value:
      --from-file <PATH|->
          Read the one value from a file, or from standard input with `-`.
          
          For a certificate, a key or a service-account JSON. At most 64 KiB of text; one final line break is dropped. The value is written on one line, in double quotes, and never printed.

      --base64
          With --from-file: store the file's bytes as base64 text (binary files)

      --keep-trailing-newline
          With --from-file: keep the file's final line break

Confirmation:
  -y, --yes
          Do not ask for confirmation

Output:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format
          
          [default: pretty]
          [possible values: pretty, json]

      --json
          Print the result as JSON: names and statuses, never a value.

Examples:
  evnx set PORT=8080                        change one line, keep the rest
  evnx set API_TOKEN                        typed with no echo, or read from stdin
  printf %s "$TOKEN" | evnx set API_TOKEN   from a pipe, nothing in shell history
  evnx set LOG_LEVEL=info --if-missing      only when the name is not there yet
  evnx set PORT=8080 --dry-run              show the change, write nothing
  evnx set TLS_KEY --from-file key.pem      a certificate or key from a file; never printed
  evnx set --fill                           ask for every empty or placeholder value
  evnx set --fill --dry-run                 list the names it would ask for; no terminal needed

Exit codes:
    0  Ran, nothing to report
    2  Could not run: usage, config, I/O, or refused before any change
    4  Declined: answered no, or a confirmation was needed with no terminal and no --yes
  141  Output closed early (broken pipe)

Full guide: https://docs.evnx.dev/cli/commands/set
